TASK A3 – DISCOVER THE DATA-ACCESS DESIGN PATTERN (5 Marks)
Bloom's Level: K4 – Analyse

Problem: A student project contains three classes: StudentDB, CourseDB and MarksDB. All three classes perform almost identical database operations. This creates code duplication and makes the application difficult to maintain.
A3.1 – Identify the Common Pattern (2 Marks)
1. Repeated Steps
The following operations are repeated in all three classes:
1. Open a database connection.
2. Prepare an SQL query.
3. Execute the SQL statement.
4. Read the ResultSet.
5. Convert database rows into Java objects.
6. Close the database connection.
2. Parts That Differ
Common operations	Different operations
Opening database connection	Table name
Executing SQL statements	Column names
Reading ResultSet	Java object type
Closing connection	Primary key
Handling SQL exceptions	Insert and update values

3. Identified Design Pattern
Data Access Object (DAO) Pattern
The DAO (Data Access Object) Pattern separates database operations from business logic and user-interface code.

Advantages:
- Reduces duplicate code.
- Improves code reusability.
- Makes database changes easier.
- Separates database logic from application logic.
- Makes testing and maintenance easier.
A3.2 – Design a Generic Data-Access Interface (2 Marks)
A generic DAO interface can be created using Java Generics.

Here, T represents the object type and K represents the primary-key type.
1. Generic DAO Interface
import java.util.List;

public interface Dao<T, K> {

    void insert(T obj);

    T findById(K id);

    List<T> findAll();

    void update(T obj);

    void delete(K id);
}


Method Explanation:
Method	Purpose
insert(T obj)	Inserts a new record
findById(K id)	Finds a record using its ID
findAll()	Retrieves all records
update(T obj)	Updates an existing record
delete(K id)	Deletes a record using its ID

